{"custom_id":"alligator__field-of-view-sharpness-dim-light-and-depth__a2","result":{"type":"succeeded","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg6kUWn1vhi8jifnPfTyC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KCmpQ4qEfcqhv7M4BnwPTZ","name":"submit_row","input":{"text":"Nobody has measured how much of the world your eyes cover, how sharply you see, how faint a light you can sense, or how you judge depth. A reflecting layer at the back of the eye, called a tapetum, has been found in the common caiman and the saltwater crocodile, and the caiman's retina is adapted to dim light. In the saltwater crocodile the sharpest vision measured is about 9 cycles a degree, against about 30 for a human, and that figure belongs to that species alone.","cites":["R1","R2","R3"],"note":"Left out R4 (six crocodilian species, severely defocused underwater) because it does not name the alligator and optics only carry within a genus."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3209,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":3484,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1071,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":771},"service_tier":"batch","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null}}}
{"custom_id":"deer__field-of-view__a2","result":{"type":"succeeded","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg6kjddSFAxpLzDZgP2FW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Hrh7up9MDbdi6XUa73Smdf","name":"submit_row","input":{"text":"Your eyes sit on the sides of your head, so each one looks out in a different direction. Nobody has measured how wide an area you see at once, or how much of it both of your eyes see together in front of you. Both have been measured in cattle, goats, giraffes and horses, but in no deer.","cites":["R10","R1","R2","R3","R4","R5","R7","R8","R9"],"note":"Left out the 310 degree figure (R10): it is an unsourced book line about deer in general, and field widths may only carry within a genus."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3357,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":3484,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":748,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":489},"service_tier":"batch","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null}}}
{"custom_id":"wader__head-movement__a2","result":{"type":"succeeded","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg6kjdksw3cDHZcGXA8ca","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0115UtR9i2wtmdQKwvfzPYVs","name":"submit_row","input":{"text":"Herons, storks and cranes bob their heads as they walk, and if you are a gray heron, little egret, intermediate egret, Pacific reef heron or Tick Bird, the most common pattern is one bob per step. If you are a Eurasian coot or a common moorhen, you bob your head while swimming as well as while walking. For most other waders, nobody has recorded how your head moves.","cites":["R25","R26","R16","R14","R13","R15","R12","R6","R7"],"note":"Fixed \"grey\" to \"gray\" and the Pacific reef heron name. Left out field-of-view figures because numbers do not carry between species."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":5748,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":3484,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1001,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":713},"service_tier":"batch","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null}}}
{"custom_id":"wader__speed__a2","result":{"type":"succeeded","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg6kkALeJWU4HdDsGF3YC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HfLB8CvCbnJsJZZcU4uDmT","name":"submit_row","input":{"text":"A lamp flashing 60 times a second looks steady to a human, but a bird's eyes may follow faster flashes. If you are a rock pigeon, you see a light flicker up to about 75 flashes a second, and if you are a domestic chicken, up to about 85. Nobody has measured how fast a flash you can follow if you are a heron, an egret, a crane or most other birds.","cites":["R1","R2"],"note":"Removed the phrase mentioning the view. Flicker speed carries only within a genus, so no heron or crane figure is stated. The chicken's 87 is rounded to 85."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2022,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":3484,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":761,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":484},"service_tier":"batch","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null}}}
{"custom_id":"reptile__the-whole-view__a2","result":{"type":"succeeded","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg6kjjcMgggHASFrJL8gi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016qMjAzVw2hLU3bWx4ixb6k","name":"submit_row","input":{"text":"If you are the turtle Trachemys scripta, you have four kinds of color-sensing cell, one of them for ultraviolet, and you see about 6 cycles a degree, where a human sees about 30. For most other reptiles, nobody has measured how sharply they see. Nobody has measured how wide an area you see at once.","cites":["R4","R6","R7"],"note":"Removed the word \"slider\" (flagged as app wording) and named the turtle by its Latin name. Left out pupil and fovea because they differ by species."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6387,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":3484,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1145,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":894},"service_tier":"batch","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null}}}
{"custom_id":"frog__movement__a2","result":{"type":"succeeded","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg6kjdH6t7FLj1JiGVxv7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01X8MQdf7ToPMTFWk5itaijq","name":"submit_row","input":{"text":"If you are a cane toad, you strike at a moving model of an insect. If you are a bullfrog, you start to avoid an object coming straight at you when it looks about 20 degrees wide to you. If you are a European toad, the nerve cells that pick out moving prey respond less when a black and white textured background moves along with the prey at the same speed. Nobody has measured how most other frogs and toads detect movement.","cites":["R1","R2","R4"],"note":"Removed the word \"view\". Left out R5 (quote does not state the result) and R3 (Japanese toad is not among the listed species)."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2456,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":3484,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":780,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":507},"service_tier":"batch","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null}}}
{"custom_id":"rabbit__the-whole-view__a2","result":{"type":"succeeded","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg6kgoMzYJ5US9eeAShVP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XeWJ7HwritJvc85hHMKppp","name":"submit_row","input":{"text":"If you are a European rabbit, you can see all the way around you when you crouch in the freeze position, with a narrow strip in front where both eyes see the same thing. You have two kinds of color-sensing cell, blue and green, and you see about a tenth of the fine detail a human sees. Nobody has measured any of this in most other rabbits, hares and pikas.","cites":["R1","R3","R4","R5","R6"],"note":"Dropped the 24 degree figure because it has no rounding that serves a job here. Left out pupil shape (R2) and tapetum (R7, R8) for length."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3821,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":3484,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":909,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":636},"service_tier":"batch","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null}}}
